### GET /blog/hello-tycho -> 200
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Hello, Tycho</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/style.css"><link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico"></head>
<body>
<nav><a href="/">Home</a> &middot; <a href="/blog">Blog</a> &middot; <a href="/wiki/getting-started">Wiki</a></nav>
<main>
<h1>Hello, Tycho</h1>
<p>Welcome to the blog. Tycho is a small systems language that compiles to C —
and the compiler itself is written in Tycho, giving byte-identical output on
every pass.</p>
<pre><code>fn main():
    println(&quot;hello, world&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>Values are copied, never aliased, so there is no <code>free</code> to call and no
collector running behind your back. Each scope's arena is reclaimed when the
scope ends. That is the whole memory model, and this post is its first demo.</p>
</main>
<footer>Served by Tycho.</footer>
</body></html>

### GET /wiki/getting-started -> 200
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Getting Started</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/style.css"><link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico"></head>
<body>
<nav><a href="/">Home</a> &middot; <a href="/blog">Blog</a> &middot; <a href="/wiki/getting-started">Wiki</a></nav>
<main>
<h1>Getting Started</h1>
<p>Build the compiler and start the example server:</p>
<pre><code>make
./tychoc examples/webserver/main.ty -o server
./server --serve
</code></pre>
<p>Then open <code>http://127.0.0.1:8080/</code> in your browser. The welcome page means
your build works.</p>
<h2>Next steps</h2>
<ul>
<li>Read the <a href="/blog/hello-tycho">Hello, Tycho</a> post.</li>
<li>Skim the language reference in <code>docs/</code>.</li>
</ul>
</main>
<footer>Served by Tycho.</footer>
</body></html>

